## Why This Is Asked
Tests pragmatic SSH latency debugging in multi-tenant contexts.

## Key Concepts
- sshd_config flags: UseDNS, GSSAPIAuthentication
- DNS resolution path and NSS order
- SSH debug with -vvv and performance timing
- Safe, minimal config changes with a clear rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js snippet to measure SSH login latency (sanity-check)
const {exec} = require('child_process');
const start = Date.now();
exec('ssh -o BatchMode=yes user@host exit', (e, stdout, stder) => {
  const latency = Date.now() - start;
  console.log('latency_ms', latency);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- If latency persists after UseDNS=no, what’s next?
- How would you validate rollback and minimize user impact?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world WAL tail latency can throttle throughput in bursts; this question probes capability to diagnose IO path bottlenecks and apply production-grade mitigations that don’t compromise data integrity.

## Key Concepts
- IO scheduling and blk-mq
- Per-disk and per-tenant I/O control (cgroups v2)
- CPU/IRQ affinity and NUMA awareness
- WAL isolation strategies and PostgreSQL tuning
- Rigorous load-testing and safe rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js: simple iostat collector for WAL latency analysis
const {spawn} = require('child_process');
const log = require('fs').createWriteStream('/var/log/iostat_wal.log', {flags:'a'});
const proc = spawn('iostat', ['-x','1']);
proc.stdout.on('data', data => log.write(data));
proc.stderr.on('data', d => process.stderr.write(d));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify data durability isn’t compromised during rollback?
- Which metrics/logs would you baseline before and after changes, and how would you automate rollback if thresholds are not met?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical, beginner-friendly plan for per-tenant log growth control without code changes; emphasizes safe rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Filesystem quotas
- logrotate
- tmpfiles.d cleanup
- Rollback strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple per-container log dir monitor (mock)
const {execSync} = require('child_process');
const threshold = 500*1024*1024;
const containers = ['ct1','ct2','ct3'];
containers.forEach(ct=>{
  const dir = `/var/log/${ct}`;
  try {
    const out = execSync(`du -sb ${dir}`).toString().trim();
    const size = parseInt(out.split('\t')[0],10);
    if(size>threshold) console.log(`${ct} over limit: ${size}`);
  } catch(e){}
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the rollback plan in a staging environment?
- What metrics would you collect to verify no regression after changes?
